></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="16" rect="493.089813,478.047119,677.016602,606.992188"
/></highlight
><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250623141954+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="39a88a0e-62c0-4746-b795-187b74a1551b" date="D:20250623142010+10'00'" name="f15de216-82a0-428c-abb9-e0e62bf9486a" icon="Comment" page="16" rect="59.555176,583.430969,83.555176,607.430969" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: These results highlight that limb loading&#xD;patterns in gymnastics are highly individualised, indicating that individual analysis is&#xD;needed to identify gymnasts (or limbs) at an increased risk of sustaining overuse injuries.&#xD;Overall, IMUs can be successfully implemented in a gymnastics training environment&#xD;and could be used in the future to prospectively monitor training loads. Training load&#xD;information can be used in conjunction with longitudinal injury data, to further investigate&#xD;the relationship between training load and injury in gymnastics, which can inform&#xD;guidelines for safe levels of loading when developing gymnastics training programmes.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="16" rect="493.227997,515.430969,677.227997,607.430969"
/></text
